  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front extending from a low pressure system \nover eastern Canada will move across our area this afternoon. Cold \nair advection, some momentum transfer from 2-3 kft AGL, and \nchanneled flow will contribute to gusts up to 25-35 mph, \nparticularly in the Mohawk Valley, Greater Capital Region, northern \nCatskills, and Berkshires. High temperatures could peak in the early \nafternoon ahead of the cold front, ranging from the high 40s to low \n50s in higher terrain and from the mid 50s to lower 60s in the \nvalleys. Some areas in the Mid-Hudson Valley could reach the mid 60s.\n\n850mb temps drop to -8C to -12C overnight Monday into Tuesday \nmorning as the mid-level trough moves overhead eastern New York and \nwestern New England. Boundary layer winds will also veer to the \nnorthwest during this period, which may lead to isolated to \nscattered lake effect rain and snow showers in the Mohawk Valley and \neastern Catskills. Snow accumulation is not expected to be \nsignificant, with a coating to a tenth of an inch possible. These \nshowers will taper off by late morning as high pressure begins to \nbuild in from the west. This high pressure will usher in mostly \nclear skies and quiet weather for the rest of Tuesday and Wednesday. \nBelow average temperatures will make a return during this period, up \nto 5 to 10 degrees F below normal. High temperatures will remain in \nthe 30s and 40s, while low temperatures drop into the 20s and teens \novernight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConfidence continues to be high for an impactful storm system\nfor Thanksgiving Day into early Friday, however there remains\nsome uncertainty regarding exact timing of precipitation onset,\nas well as precipitation type and amounts. Guidance continues\ntrend of slower precipitation onset, reaching areas near and\nsouth of I-90 late Thursday morning or early afternoon, and\nperhaps not until late afternoon or evening across portions of\nthe southern Adirondacks. With a later start time, chances for\nP-type to be plain rain, even at the start, have increased with\nthe exception of the highest elevations of the southern\nAdirondacks and southern VT where some snow or sleet could be\nmixed in initially.\n\nAbundant moisture associated with the storm system should allow\nfor a widespread soaking rainfall for most areas. 01Z/23 NBM\n48-hour probs for > 1 inch of precip is 50-90%, except 40-60%\nacross portions of the Hudson River Valley, Taconics and Mohawk\nValley where downsloping may reduce amounts within a strong\neast/southeast low level flow regime.\n\nIn addition, strong east/southeast winds are expected across \nsome higher terrain areas of western New England given steep low\nlevel pressure gradient between strong departing high pressure \nand approaching low. 01Z/23 NBM 24-hour probs for max wind \ngusts > 40 mph ending 1 AM Friday are as high as 40-75% across \nportions of the Taconics, Berkshires, Litchfield Hills and \nsouthern Greens. Some of these areas may eventually need wind\nadvisories should confidence remain high for these wind gust\nmagnitudes to occur, as this would lead to an increased \npotential for some downed tree limbs/trees and some power \noutages in these areas.",
